face with assassination.” (If true, this shows either remarkable forbearance 
on Toledos part, or serious cowardice. In either case, it shows how freely the 
man could be abused.) 

Among military men, however, Garcia de Toledo ranks high and always has, 
When the twenty-two-year-old Don John of Austria was tapped to command 
the Holy League prior to the battle of Lepanto, he actively sought out advice 
from the aging veteran. Brantéme wrote a glowing encomium.”™ In the later 
nineteenth century, Jurien de la Graviére, himself an admiral in the French navy 
and a veteran of Sebastopol, expressed some sympathy for the man’s impossible 
position.” Twentieth-century historians have tended to follow suit — Braudel 
comes down firmly in his camp, and Guilmartin lays out the defense of his 
action in some detail in his Gunpowder and Galleys.”° 


He died quietly in Naples in 1577. 


